From cb5f7a27ccbc376614268adffcd7d3d76ca964d2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Guilhem Bichot <guilhem.bichot@contributor>

Date: Wed, 6 Aug 2025 17:03:04 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] enable plan_cache, set differents values and
 execute a query which contains conditions of time and timestamp type, the
 result returned for the second time is wrong.

As you can see in the last two EXPLAIN added to the test in the
dstore_main suite, the first one and the second one use a different
index. Due to the very special value of @col2 in the first one, the
BETWEEN on timestamp_col is actually an equality, whereas in the
second case, it is a "true" range, and probably this causes different
cost values, and a different choice of index - but the reasons are
irrelevant anyway. Note that the first uses ICP, the second does not.

In the bug report, with plan cache on, and without the code fix, we
saw an ICP in BOTH. Here is what happened.

In the plan cache code, the ICP is cached the first time and
unconditionally re-applied the second time (however the constant in
the comparison in the ICP condition in the second case is properly the
new value of @col2 - that particular point is no issue).

Plan cache does not cache the range scan object (the thing produced by
test_quick_select()), it only caches the index's number (see
Exec_context::fill()) and the range scan's type (index merge, etc),
and when applying the cached plan at the 2nd execution, it applies the
cached ICP condition, then calls the range optimizer to create a fresh
range scan, with hints to try to force it to pick the cached type and
cached index ; if the range optimizer fails to create a range scan
with these constraints (and it is the case here, probably as the
range's shape changed?), it invalidates the cached plan and goes back
to normal optimization. The mistake is that, when invalidating, it
forgets to clean up the ICP condition which it had applied!

Fix: when we apply the cached plan, including the applying of a cached
ICP, if we later decide to invalidate the plan after all, cancel ICP.
While at it, we improve two things:
- at the cancellation place, also cancel more members (again to undo
the effects of the failed applying): covering_keys, key_read, and the
hint for the range optimizer.
- at the place where we apply ICP: instead of setting table->file
members directly, now go through the handler API idx_cond_push(); the
benefit is to make the "cached plan" execution path as identical as
possible to the "normal" path which calls idx_cond_push() too.
Likewise, instead of setting these members to "none", call
cancel_pushed_idx_cond() (like handler::ha_reset()) which takes care
of resetting one more member and can be overriden in engines.
